{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1092446-advice-on-how-to-do-a-indiana-jones-style-storyline","id":1092446,"title":"Advice on how to do a Indiana Jones style storyline .","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-14T13:04:46-07:00","id":1092446,"title":"Advice on how to do a Indiana Jones style storyline .","content":"I had a idea recently. My idea was to combine two things I am interested in, that being Egyptology and Indiana Jones, and making it into a Mage storyline . The basic story is that you are a group of Mage's in Jacksonville, Fl who get swept up in a adventure that takes you all the way to the tomb of a ancient mage.<br /> <br /> The main antagonists are the Cordov Brothers, Russian billionaires who run a corporation called Staghorn Excavation . The brothers are after a powerful magical artifact hidden within the tomb of a dead Egyptian mage . It is unknown why, but the prevalent theory is that the Brothers are really the patsys of either a ambitious Technocrat or a bloodthirsty Nephandi. During this globetrotting adventure you will run into technocratic agents, Staghorn Employees and thugs hired by the Cordov brothers <br /> <br /> I want help on detailing the antagonist's, their mysterious employer and the artifact itself","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12186-killerclown","name":"Killerclown","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"819"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-14T14:22:06-07:00","id":1092494,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't worry about the artifact too much less you want to give it to the players early on in the game, I would either keep it as a MacGuffin or intrical to the end game of the plot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T02:03:35-07:00","id":1092703,"title":"","content":"You may, alternatively, make the mysterious employer a Follower of Set. They have plenty of blood sorcerers, who might have interest in magical ancient Egyptian artifacts.<br /> <br /> If the cabal is being made for this adventure (rather than being a preexisting group), consider pushing your players towards characters of the Society of Ether, Order of Hermes, and/or Ngoma. The latter two groups have ties to Egyptian high ritual magick, and are generally scholarly. The Etherites, meanwhile, have a faction called the Adventurers, who take inspiration from pulp era adventure, crime, and SF stories. A genre from which Indiana Jones <i>also</i> took inspiration. That group is also one of the major Etherite factions willing to pursue even mystical secrets, which they see as potential sources for Scientific discovery (&quot;<a href=\"http://www.girlgeniusonline.com/comic.php?date=20081205#.WRluWYjyvIU\" target=\"_blank\">Any sufficiently analyzed magic is indistinguishable from Science!</a>&quot;).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T07:40:38-07:00","id":1092800,"title":"","content":"For the artifact, it might depend on how you're intending to use it.<br /> <br /> For example, if the artifact is intended to be some kind of Wonder, then it might be worth looking at Ancient Egyptian folklore or religion to see if you can find some inspiration on what exactly you want it to do. You might wish to associate it with a particular deity and give it a power based on that deity's domain. For example, an artifact that is associated with Hathor might increase attraction or enable an infertile person to have children. <br /> <br /> Or the artifact itself may not be mystical in nature, but could have either financial, religious, or sentimental value. <br /> <br /> If you're going with the idea of the artifact having some kind of power, I'd think very carefully about it. It ought to be worth the effort, but you should probably operate under the assumption that the PCs will get their hands on it. So you'll likely want to avoid anything TOO powerful, that you don't want to deal with them having later.<br /> <br /> The Followers of Set could be a great choice for adversaries. (You could bring in the Bubasti as well?) Another option might be to use Bane Mummies, though that might be tricky unless you were bringing in some NPC allies for the players as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T10:09:22-07:00","id":1092853,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1092446-advice-on-how-to-do-a-indiana-jones-style-storyline?p=1092800#post1092800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you're going with the idea of the artifact having some kind of power, I'd think very carefully about it. It ought to be worth the effort, but you should probably operate under the assumption that the PCs will get their hands on it. So you'll likely want to avoid anything TOO powerful, that you don't want to deal with them having later.</div> </div> </div> </div>Alternatively, you could have the artifact be VERY VERY powerful (as in, &quot;up to ST fiat&quot; level abilities), but insure that using it has serious consequences. Better yet, serious PERSONAL consequences for the user.<br /> <br /> Like, I dunno, using the Ark of Covenant and getting your face melted.... Just as an example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}